                  "role": "assistant",
                  "content": "# Hash Tables: Mechanics and Collision Resolution\n\n## Core Concept\n\nA **hash table** (or hash map) is a data structure that implements an associative array, mapping **keys** to **values** using a **hash function**. It provides average-case **O(1)** time complexity for insert, delete, and lookup operations.\n\n### How It Works\n\n```\nKey \u2192 Hash Function \u2192 Hash Value \u2192 Index \u2192 Bucket \u2192 Value\n```\n\n1. **Hash Function**: Takes a key and produces a fixed-size integer (hash code).\n2. **Index Calculation**: The hash code is mapped to an array index, typically via modulo:\n   ```\n   index = hash(key) % table_size\n   ```\n3. **Storage**: The key-value pair is stored at the computed index (bucket).\n4. **Retrieval**: The same process is repeated to locate the bucket, then the key is compared to confirm a match.\n\n### Simple Example\n\n```python\n# Conceptual illustration\ntable_size = 10\n\ndef hash_function(key):\n    return sum(ord(c) for c in key) % table_size\n\n# Insert \"apple\" -> 42\nindex = hash_function(\"apple\")  # e.g",
